# dist/ ships to every platform from whatever machine cut the release, and its
# byte-level hashes are recorded in SHA256SUMS.txt. Every text file in it must
# therefore check out IDENTICALLY everywhere — the release workflow's
# `sha256sum -c` gate runs on a Linux checkout, and an unpinned .txt that
# normalised differently there fails the gate on content that is "the same".
# LF for everything first; the later, more specific rules (*.bat crlf, the
# binary entries) override where they must.
dist/**             text eol=lf
scripts/dist-docs/* text eol=lf

# Executable text must keep its native line endings REGARDLESS of the checkout
# platform. A shell script checked out with CRLF dies on its first line
# ("set: pipefail: invalid option name" — the option was 'pipefail\r'), and a
# release packaged from such a checkout ships an install.sh that no Linux or
# macOS machine can run. Batch files have the mirrored problem.
*.sh      text eol=lf
gradlew   text eol=lf
*.py      text eol=lf
*.bat     text eol=crlf

# The engine patch series must apply with `git am` byte-for-byte.
*.patch   -text

# Binaries, never normalised.
*.jar     binary
*.png     binary
*.jpg     binary
*.zip     binary
br-sidecar     binary
br-sidecar.exe binary
